\section{Three descriptions of the same bytes}

A relocatable ELF object describes its contents primarily as \emph{sections}. A compiler and assembler put instructions, constants, writable data, and bookkeeping into sections such as \texttt{.text}, \texttt{.rodata}, \texttt{.data}, and \texttt{.symtab}. The static linker combines input sections, places output sections, and constructs program headers describing the resulting \emph{segments}. A running process, in turn, has page-granular \emph{virtual mappings}. A segment is a request encoded in the file; a mapping is the address-space state that implements all or part of that request. Neither the line count in a memory map nor the count of sections tells us the number of loadable segments.

Levine makes the distinction explicit in his description of ELF storage allocation \cite[chapter~4]{levine2000linkers}. His compact 32-bit example maps one file page twice, once for the end of text and once for the beginning of data, at different virtual addresses and with different permissions \cite[chapter~3]{levine2000linkers}. That is a possible layout, not a prescription for two segments or executable data. Current GNU toolchains on Linux commonly emit separate read-only, executable, and writable ranges. A single \texttt{PT\_LOAD} can contain several sections or bytes, such as the ELF header, that belong to no section. Conversely, nonallocated debugging sections need not appear in any mapping. The runtime linking metadata is also located through program headers, not through the section names printed by a file inspector \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}.

\section{Offsets, addresses, and page permissions}

In a loadable program header, \texttt{p\_offset} is a file offset; \texttt{p\_vaddr} is a link-time virtual address. Write $B$ for the load bias: a link-time address $v$ in this image corresponds to runtime address $B+v$. A conventional Linux \texttt{ET\_EXEC} main image uses its linked addresses, whereas a PIE can be \texttt{ET\_DYN} with a nonzero bias chosen at execution. Each loadable segment in a given image uses the same bias. Applying a different bias to each segment would break distances that the static linker already fixed.

Let $F$ and $M$ be the file and memory sizes. ELF requires $F\leq M$. The first $F$ bytes come from the file; the remaining $M-F$ bytes start as zero. Loadable offsets and virtual addresses are congruent modulo the page size, as the AMD64 ABI's program-loading discussion explains. A nontrivial \texttt{p\_align} additionally specifies alignment as a power of two, with corresponding offset/address congruence. Rounding down the file and virtual starts therefore preserves the translation between initialized bytes. The \texttt{PF\_R}, \texttt{PF\_W}, and \texttt{PF\_X} flags describe requested permissions, applied by the operating system at page granularity; neighboring bytes on one virtual page cannot independently have different permissions.

