\section{Plan an initialized range and its zero tail}

For a teaching model, take one writable load segment with $F>0$, $M>F$, a page-aligned bias, and no overlapping virtual pages. Validate file bounds and arithmetic before making the plan. Let \texttt{down} and \texttt{up} round to page boundaries; all ranges below are half-open. This models the result for the segment, not the order of Linux kernel calls.

\begin{verbatim}
o = p_offset; a = B + p_vaddr
f = a + p_filesz; m = a + p_memsz
require o mod page == a mod page   # preserve byte positions
map [down(a), up(f)) privately from file offset down(o)
clear [f, min(m, up(f)))           # discard unwanted file bytes
map [up(f), up(m)) as zero pages   # no file payload for this part
\end{verbatim}

Use 4 KiB pages and hexadecimal values. Let $B=\texttt{0x400000}$, the file offset be \texttt{0x2800}, the link address be \texttt{0x3800}, and $F,M$ be \texttt{0x900}, \texttt{0x2500}. Both starts have low bits \texttt{0x800}. Map file pages starting at \texttt{0x2000} into \texttt{[0x403000,0x405000)}. The initialized bytes occupy \texttt{[0x403800,0x404100)}. Clearing the part-page interval ending at \texttt{0x405000} and adding a zero page up to \texttt{0x406000} supplies zeros through the logical end \texttt{0x405d00}, exclusive. The page-rounded padding beyond that end is not part of the segment's logical payload.

For every initialized byte at offset $i<F$, the invariant is that runtime address $a+i$ has the original file byte at $o+i$ before program writes. Test the boundary with nonzero fixture bytes at file offsets \texttt{0x30ff} and \texttt{0x3100}: the first is preserved at \texttt{0x4040ff}; the second does not appear at \texttt{0x404100}, which must read as zero. The same file need not be changed on disk: a write to a private file-backed mapping can create a private copy of the page.

