\section{Inspect a linker-produced image}

Compile \texttt{unsigned char sample[8192];} together with \texttt{int main(void)\{return sample[0];\}} using \texttt{cc -fPIE -pie map.c -o map}. Use \texttt{readelf -SW map} for sections and \texttt{readelf -lW map} for program headers and their section-to-segment correspondence. Find \texttt{.bss} with type \texttt{NOBITS} and locate it in a writable \texttt{LOAD} whose memory size exceeds its file size. Do not expect the difference to equal exactly 8192: alignment and other zero-initialized objects can contribute. In a running process, a zero tail can split into a private file-backed part and an anonymous part, and later permission changes can further split a range. These are differences between views of one image, not evidence that the loader invented more sections.

Mapping a range establishes what the process will observe when it accesses those addresses, not that every page has already been read. Demand paging can defer reads, and two processes mapping the same unmodified file bytes can share physical pages even when their virtual addresses differ.
